{
	// Cloudflare Worker config for the core-api service — the `/api/v1/*` and
	// `/xrpc/*` surface that runs on Cloud Run today.
	//
	// The repo now has TWO Workers, which is why this file exists rather than
	// the root one growing a second entry: a wrangler config describes exactly
	// one Worker. The root `wrangler.jsonc` stays the docs site's assets-only
	// Worker, because the Cloudflare dashboard's "Connect to Git" build for it
	// runs a bare `npx wrangler deploy` at the repo root and would find this one
	// only if that command changed. Deploy this one explicitly:
	//
	//     npx wrangler deploy -c apps/core-api/wrangler.jsonc
	//
	// See specs/cloudflare-migration.md § Sequencing, step 3.
	"$schema": "../../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
	"name": "antiphony-core-api",
	"main": "src/worker.ts",

	// `nodejs_compat` is not optional here. `node:crypto` backs three things on
	// the request path — `constantTimeEqual` (service-token comparison),
	// `idempotency.ts`'s hashing, and the webhook notifier's HMAC — and the
	// flag is what provides it. The compat DATE matters separately: from
	// 2025-04-01 the runtime populates `process.env` from vars and secrets,
	// which is what lets `app-config.ts`, `service-auth.ts`, and the provider
	// registry keep reading `process.env` on both runtimes instead of growing a
	// second config path.
	"compatibility_date": "2025-11-01",
	"compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

	// ## Smart Placement, on from the first deploy — not after measuring
	//
	// Workers run close to the USER. Neon is single-region on AWS `us-east-1`,
	// and the Cloud Run deployment this replaces is in `us-east4` — the same
	// metro, so database round trips are currently single-digit milliseconds.
	// Moving to the edge is therefore a latency REGRESSION by default: a
	// request served from London against a database in Virginia pays ~80ms per
	// round trip, times however many sequential queries the handler makes.
	//
	// Smart Placement relocates execution close to the backend when a Worker
	// makes several round trips to one origin, which is exactly this workload's
	// shape. It is on from the start because the regression it prevents is
	// INVISIBLE when testing from the US east coast — the one place the two
	// topologies look identical. See specs/cloudflare-migration.md § Geography.
	"placement": { "mode": "smart" },

	"observability": { "enabled": true },

	// ## The TTL sweep finally gets a caller
	//
	// `antiphony_sweep_expired()` has shipped with `db/schema.sql` since step 2
	// with nothing calling it. It replaces the native Firestore TTL on
	// `idempotency_keys` and `rate_limits`, which Postgres has no equivalent
	// for, so without a trigger both tables grow forever.
	//
	// Hourly, at a minute that is not :00, because every cron in the world
	// fires on the hour and there is no reason to join them. It is deliberately
	// not `pg_cron`: Neon runs those jobs only while the compute is awake, and
	// a beta service is idle nearly always — which is precisely when scale to
	// zero should be on. A trigger outside the database fires regardless of
	// compute state, and waking Neon is the feature rather than the
	// precondition. See schema.sql § Why NOT pg_cron.
	"triggers": { "crons": ["17 * * * *"] },

	// ## Audio processing — producer and consumer are the same Worker
	//
	// `PROCESSING_QUEUE` replaces Cloud Tasks. The consumer is this script's
	// `queue()` handler, so there is no worker URL to configure and no
	// `SYSTEM_AUTH_TOKEN` stored per task the way Cloud Tasks had to.
	//
	// ### `max_batch_size` is 1, and that is a derivation, not a default
	//
	// One message is one full processing pass: a denoise call to ElevenLabs
	// over a multi-megabyte upload, then transcription. The whole BATCH shares
	// one consumer invocation, and that invocation is capped at 15 minutes — so
	// a batch of N is N passes racing one 15-minute budget, and blowing it
	// retries every message in the batch, including the ones that already
	// succeeded. Billable stages would be re-billed.
	//
	// Memory says the same thing independently: `readBlobBytes` materialises a
	// whole blob, the upload route caps at 25MB, and a Worker isolate has
	// 128MB. Several of those at once is the one place that ceiling is close.
	//
	// So concurrency belongs BETWEEN invocations, where Cloudflare scales
	// consumers horizontally on its own, not within one.
	//
	// ### Retries
	//
	// Only a pass that THREW is handed back — see lib/process-audio-job.ts for
	// why that is the sole retryable outcome. Three attempts, then the dead
	// letter queue, which must exist before this deploys:
	//
	//     npx wrangler queues create antiphony-processing
	//     npx wrangler queues create antiphony-processing-dlq
	"queues": {
		"producers": [
			{
				"binding": "PROCESSING_QUEUE",
				"queue": "antiphony-processing"
			}
		],
		"consumers": [
			{
				"queue": "antiphony-processing",
				"max_batch_size": 1,
				// Irrelevant at batch size 1 beyond "do not wait to fill a
				// batch that is already full", which is what a low value says.
				"max_batch_timeout": 5,
				"max_retries": 3,
				"dead_letter_queue": "antiphony-processing-dlq"
			}
		]
	},

	// ## Rate-limit buckets
	//
	// One Durable Object per bucket key, addressed with `idFromName(key)`. This
	// is the one table where Postgres was actively the wrong tool: a
	// high-frequency counter write sitting on the READ path, so a network hop
	// to a single-region database means every rate-limited request pays a round
	// trip to Virginia before the handler starts.
	//
	// Per-key strongly-consistent counters are what Durable Objects are for,
	// and input gating makes the read-increment-write atomic for free — where
	// the Firestore binding needed a transaction plus a whole contention branch
	// to get the same guarantee.
	//
	// The `rate_limits` table in db/schema.sql was always a bridge rather than
	// a destination (its own comment says so). It stays, harmlessly, for a
	// deployment without this binding attached.
	//
	// `migrations` is not optional: a Durable Object class is only addressable
	// once a migration has declared it. `new_sqlite_classes` rather than
	// `new_classes` because SQLite-backed is the current default storage
	// backend and the only one available on new namespaces.
	"durable_objects": {
		"bindings": [
			{
				"name": "RATE_LIMITER",
				"class_name": "RateLimiter"
			}
		]
	},
	"migrations": [
		{
			"tag": "v1",
			"new_sqlite_classes": ["RateLimiter"]
		}
	],

	// ## The shared layer of the app-DID custody cache
	//
	// `middleware/auth.ts` proves each tenant's pin before a handler runs. That
	// check is isolate-local first; this namespace is the layer underneath it,
	// so a COLD isolate does not re-resolve a `did:web` document another
	// isolate proved a minute ago.
	//
	// KV's eventual consistency is fine here and would not be for
	// `idempotency_keys` — the difference is what a stale read costs. A stale
	// idempotency answer breaks the guarantee outright; a stale custody proof
	// is bounded by design, since the whole point of the layer below it is that
	// an old proof is still the right thing to serve when the DID host is
	// unreachable. The value carries its own `validatedAt` and the freshness
	// and staleness bounds are enforced in code, not by the store.
	//
	//     npx wrangler kv namespace create PIN_CACHE
	"kv_namespaces": [
		{
			"binding": "PIN_CACHE",
			"id": "REPLACE_ME_WITH_wrangler_kv_namespace_create_OUTPUT"
		}
	],

	// Audio blobs and their derived renditions. One bucket, two prefixes:
	// `blobs/{originAppId}/{cid}` and `renditions/{originAppId}/{cid}.{format}`.
	//
	// Binding-based authorisation is why there is no R2 credential anywhere in
	// this config — and why dropping `getSignedUrl` made the blob store simpler
	// rather than harder. See specs/cloudflare-migration.md § Secrets.
	"r2_buckets": [
		{
			"binding": "BLOBS",
			"bucket_name": "antiphony-r2-bucket"
		}
	],

	// ## Hyperdrive, not a DATABASE_URL secret
	//
	// The Worker reads `env.HYPERDRIVE.connectionString`, which is a LOCAL
	// pooled string — not the Neon credential. That is the whole point: the
	// database never becomes a Worker secret, and nothing can read one back out
	// of Cloudflare anyway.
	//
	// `id` is per-account and comes from `wrangler hyperdrive create`, so it
	// cannot be committed meaningfully — the placeholder below has to be
	// replaced before the first deploy, and `wrangler deploy` fails on it
	// rather than starting up without a database. `readRuntimeEnv` still
	// accepts a raw `DATABASE_URL` if Hyperdrive is ever skipped, but
	// § Geography argues it should not be: the connection setup Hyperdrive
	// removes is a full extra round trip to Virginia on every cold path.
	"hyperdrive": [
		{
			"binding": "HYPERDRIVE",
			"id": "REPLACE_ME_WITH_wrangler_hyperdrive_create_OUTPUT"
		}
	],

	// Non-secret configuration. Everything else — SYSTEM_AUTH_TOKEN,
	// ANTIPHONY_APP_TOKENS, ELEVENLABS_API_KEY — is a Worker SECRET, set with
	// `wrangler secret put`, and must never appear in this file.
	//
	// ANTIPHONY_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is required, not cosmetic: since the audio
	// proxy streams bytes rather than redirecting to a signed URL,
	// `AudioEmbedView.url` is an absolute URL pointing back at this service.
	// Without it a post with audio hydrates with no embed at all.
	//
	// ANTIPHONY_APP_DIDS is a var rather than a secret: DIDs are public by
	// construction, and keeping it here is what lets the CI deploy gate
	// (`npm run validate:pins`) prove the pins in the config about to ship
	// without needing access to anything secret.
	"vars": {
		"ANTIPHONY_PUBLIC_BASE_URL": "https://api.antiphony.dev",
		"ANTIPHONY_R2_BUCKET": "antiphony-r2-bucket",
		"ANTIPHONY_PDS_HOST": "api.antiphony.dev",
		"ANTIPHONY_APP_DIDS": "voxpop:did:web:did.voxpop.audio",
		// The transcode backend for on-demand renditions (apps/audio-rendition).
		// Absent is a supported state: the audio proxy then serves renditions
		// that already exist and 404s the rest, which is what a deployment
		// pre-warming everything it needs wants. Reached with SYSTEM_AUTH_TOKEN,
		// so both must be present or neither — a URL with no token is a
		// rendition path that looks configured and 401s on every miss.
		"ANTIPHONY_RENDITION_SERVICE_URL": "https://antiphony-audio-rendition-REPLACE_ME.us-east4.run.app",
		"NODE_ENV": "production",
		"LOG_LEVEL": "info"
	}
}
